description:
**File:** rust/lichen-schc/src/fragment.rs
**Lines:** 98-113

**Issue:** The `compute_mic` function implements CRC-32/ISO-HDLC but the code lacks documentation explaining the threat model:
```rust
/// CRC-32/ISO-HDLC over the SCHC Packet followed by the All-1 zero pad bit,
/// byte-extended as one zero octet.
pub fn compute_mic(data: &[u8]) -> [u8; MIC_LENGTH] {
```

CRC-32 provides error detection against random bit flips, NOT protection against malicious modification. An attacker can trivially compute valid CRCs for arbitrary payloads.

**Trigger:** A reader might assume "MIC" implies cryptographic authentication when it does not.

**Context:** Per RFC 8724, SCHC fragmentation relies on OSCORE for authentication. The CRC-32 MIC is for detecting transmission errors only.

**Suggested fix:** Add security documentation:
```rust
/// CRC-32/ISO-HDLC over the SCHC Packet followed by the All-1 zero pad bit,
/// byte-extended as one zero octet.
///
/// SECURITY: This CRC-32 checksum ("MIC" per RFC 8724) provides error detection
/// against random bit corruption only. It provides NO protection against malicious
/// modification - an attacker can trivially compute valid CRCs. Authentication
/// is provided by OSCORE at a higher layer. Do not rely on this MIC for security.
pub fn compute_mic(data: &[u8]) -> [u8; MIC_LENGTH] {
```
